declare(strict_types=1);
echo '<div class="mk-prose">';
echo '<h2>Key Topics in Igbo History</h2>';

echo '<h3>1. Igbo-Ukwu: The Evidence That Changed Everything</h3>';
echo '<p>In 1938, a man digging a cistern in Igbo-Ukwu village in what is now Anambra State uncovered bronze objects of extraordinary quality. The archaeologist Thurstan Shaw excavated the site in 1959–1960 and 1964, uncovering three separate deposits: a burial chamber containing the remains of a man of high status surrounded by regalia; a storehouse of ritual objects; and a pit containing more than 700 objects. The bronze castings — produced by the lost-wax process — were of a sophistication comparable to the finest contemporary bronzes produced anywhere in the world. Radiocarbon dating placed them in the 9th century CE.</p>';
echo '<p>The Igbo-Ukwu finds are significant in multiple ways. They demonstrate high metallurgical and artistic achievement in the region at least a thousand years before European contact. They show evidence of long-distance trade — the glass beads found at the site originated in Egypt or India, indicating that the Niger Delta region was connected to Indian Ocean and trans-Saharan trading networks in the 9th century. And they provided the first direct archaeological evidence that precolonial Igbo society had achieved the kind of ritual elaboration and material sophistication that colonial historiography had denied to sub-Saharan Africa generally and to the "stateless" Igbo specifically.</p>';

echo '<h3>2. The Nri Kingdom: Power Without Conquest</h3>';
echo '<p>The Kingdom of Nri is one of the oldest polities in West Africa and one of the most distinctive in the world. Founded, according to Nri tradition, by Eri — a figure who descended from the sky and whose descendants became the Eze Nri — the kingdom exercised authority across a wide area of Igboland not through military force but through ritual service. The Eze Nri\'s priests performed <em>ifi</em> — purification rites that cleansed communities of abominations (<em>nso</em>) — and consecrated the <em>ozo</em> and <em>nze</em> title systems that structured social hierarchy across Igboland. Communities invited Nri priests to perform these services; the invitation itself acknowledged Nri\'s authority.</p>';
echo '<p>This is a form of political power that has no good parallel in European political theory — which is perhaps why colonial administrators systematically misunderstood and misrepresented it. Nri did not tax, did not conscript, did not conquer. It provided ritual services that communities needed and could not provide for themselves. In exchange, it received recognition, hospitality, and the social authority that came from being the source of legitimate titles. It is a model of distributed, consensual authority that the Igbo political tradition has expressed in different forms across its history — and that makes the Igbo experience of centralised Nigerian statehood so persistently problematic.</p>';

echo '<h3>3. The Women\'s War of 1929: Democracy in Action</h3>';
echo '<p>The Women\'s War — <em>Ogu Umunwanyi</em> — of November–December 1929 remains the largest anti-colonial uprising in Nigerian history and one of the most remarkable demonstrations of collective political action in 20th-century African history. Triggered by rumours that the British colonial administration intended to extend direct taxation to women, hundreds of thousands of women across Owerri and Calabar Provinces mobilised in coordinated protest, using the traditional practice of "sitting on a man" — surrounding the compound of an offender, singing, dancing, and demanding redress.</p>';
echo '<p>The uprising had no single leader. It was coordinated through the existing networks of Igbo women\'s associations, market organisations, and age-grade groups — the same institutions that the colonial administration had dismissed as irrelevant because they did not fit the European model of political authority. The women attacked Native Administration courts — the symbols of British indirect rule — destroyed court buildings, released prisoners, and forced the resignation of warrant chiefs. The British response was armed suppression: 55 women were killed at Aba and Utu Etim Ekpo. The Aba Commission of Inquiry that followed acknowledged the legitimacy of the women\'s grievances and recommended the abolition of the warrant chief system. The women won the political argument. They paid with their lives.</p>';

echo '<h3>4. The Biafra War: What It Was and What It Was Not</h3>';
echo '<p>The Nigeria-Biafra War has been interpreted in multiple ways: as a failed secession, as an ethnic conflict, as a Cold War proxy struggle, as a humanitarian catastrophe, as a war of survival. Each of these interpretations captures something real. What none of them captures adequately is the Igbo experience of the war — the experience of a people who had been massacred in their hundreds of thousands in the north, who had fled south in the largest internal displacement in Nigerian history to that point, who had watched a peace accord (Aburi, January 1967) negotiated in good faith be repudiated by the federal government within weeks, and who understood Biafra not as an act of aggression but as the only available response to a state that had demonstrated it could not or would not protect them.</p>';
echo '<p>The federal blockade — cutting off food, medicine, and supplies to a civilian population of approximately 14 million people — was a deliberate strategy. Obafemi Awolowo, then federal Finance Commissioner, articulated the logic: starvation was a legitimate weapon of war. The images of kwashiorkor-afflicted Biafran children that reached the world in 1968–1969 produced the first modern humanitarian media crisis and the founding of Médecins Sans Frontières. Between 500,000 and 2 million people died of starvation. The war ended in January 1970 with Biafra\'s military collapse. The starvation, the abandoned property, the twenty-pound bank limit, the federal reconstruction that bypassed the Igbo heartland — these were the peace.</p>';

echo '<h3>5. The Igbo Diaspora: Global Presence, Unresolved Questions</h3>';
echo '<p>The contemporary Igbo world is simultaneously local and global. Within Nigeria, Igbo communities maintain the social institutions — town unions, age-grade associations, women\'s organisations, Pentecostal churches — that have structured Igbo political and social life for centuries, adapted to urban and post-war conditions. Outside Nigeria, the Igbo diaspora is one of the most geographically dispersed and institutionally organised African diasporas in the world, with significant communities in the United Kingdom, the United States, Canada, continental Europe, and across Africa.</p>';
echo '<p>The diaspora reproduces Igbo social institutions in new contexts: Ohanaeze branches in London and Houston; town development unions raising money for projects in home communities; Igbo language schools in Manchester and Atlanta; IPOB mobilisation through WhatsApp groups and Radio Biafra broadcasts. The unresolved questions of the civil war — justice, memory, sovereignty, the structural marginalisation of the Igbo within the Nigerian federal state — animate diaspora politics as intensely as they animate politics in the southeast. The Igbo diaspora is not a footnote to Igbo history. It is one of its primary current expressions.</p>';
